.TH CHMAP_DESTROY_WITH_DTOR 3 "2026-08-19" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_destroy_with_dtor \- destroy a hash map, running a destructor on every value first, without allocating memory
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

void chmap_destroy_with_dtor(chmap chm,
    void (*val_dtor)(const cmap_pair *val_pair, void *dtor_ctx),
    void *dtor_ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Destroys the map exactly as chmap_destroy(3) does. There is one
difference. When val_dtor is not NULL, this function calls it once for
each live entry. It gives that entry's own value as a cmap_pair. The
call happens directly before the function frees the entry.

This function never allocates memory of its own. It walks the map's own
internal storage directly, and the destructor call runs inside that same
walk. A walk with chashmap_begin_iter(3), and a destroy after it, does
allocate.

This function is for a map whose values are owned pointers to a larger
structure. Such a structure needs its own recursive teardown, for
example a dictionary of dictionaries. There is another way to destroy
such a map. Walk it with chashmap_begin_iter(3), run a value destructor
for each entry, and destroy the empty map at the end. That way has a
real gap under memory pressure. The small internal allocation of
chashmap_begin_iter(3) can itself fail. That way then has no route left
to reach and free the values that the map still owns. This function has
no such gap, because it does no allocation of its own.

The cmap_pair that val_dtor gets is the map's own accessor for that
entry. Its target is const, as in chmap_get_elem_ref(3). A destructor
reads val_pair->ptr and val_pair->size. It then frees whatever the value
itself owns. That is usually a pointer copied out of those bytes. An
assignment to val_pair->ptr or val_pair->size is a compile error.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm        Hash map to destroy
val_dtor   Destructor called once for each live value, before the
           function frees that entry. Use NULL to destroy the map with
           no callback for each value. Its val_pair parameter is a
           const cmap_pair *
dtor_ctx   Opaque pointer forwarded unchanged to every val_dtor call
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call with a NULL chm (a no-op)
Does not set the chm handle to NULL. Use chmap_destroy(3) when the
handle must be cleared as well
val_dtor must not change chm itself. The map is in the middle of its
teardown for the whole duration of this call
The accessor describes storage that the map is about to free. The map
cannot own a pointer that it did not allocate itself. This is why an
assignment to either field of the accessor is a compile error, and not
only a rule to remember
Does no allocation of its own. It therefore cannot fail under any
memory condition that makes chashmap_begin_iter(3) itself fail
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_destroy (3),
.BR chmap_get_elem_ref (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3)
